{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1520401933,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3MTAzMDk3MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2275#issuecomment-371030970"},"message":"I think it is very useful to record/use the parent shell. I am not so sure that a dedicated `--shell` would see much use. The primary pattern would be to specify the shell that one uses anyways, and then we have the exact same \"complicate re-execution\" situation we already have.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1520402018,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3MTAzMTI0MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2275#issuecomment-371031240"},"message":"FTR: the idea of a `run --interactive` from #2158 is somewhat related, as a solution for this issue could also qualify as a solution for that case.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1520432928,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3MTE1NTIxOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2275#issuecomment-371155219"},"message":"Few bullet points\n- parent shell is only useful if it is the shell used within the `run`.  So I think we should record what shell was **used** if not a `/bin/sh`.  \n- as for `--shell` -- I am using `zsh` but really what I `run` is typically `/bin/bash` so could be ran on machines even without `zsh` which would be forced upon for `rerun` if I record `zsh`.\n- we might actually want to make `run` to allow for non-shell execution (although I do not think I have a handy use-case ATM, besides pointing to all the warnings of security danger of shell=True ;))\n- note that both `bash` and `zsh` known to change through time, so we ideally should also then record their versions, but that places us into all that tracing/provenance collection domain which we might want to avoid for now.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1520437576,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3MTE4MTQwNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2275#issuecomment-371181406"},"message":"\u003e besides pointing to all the warnings of security danger of shell=True\n\nI don't  understand the concern.  Isn't the main worry just shell injection?  To use the [example from the subprocess docs][0]:\n\n```python\n\u003e\u003e\u003e from subprocess import call\n\u003e\u003e\u003e filename = input(\"What file would you like to display?\\n\")\nWhat file would you like to display?\nnon_existent; rm -rf / #\n\u003e\u003e\u003e call(\"cat \" + filename, shell=True) # Uh-oh. This will end badly...\n```\n\nSure, that's bad because the intention is to just cat a file.  But in our case, we're saying \"we're running what you're giving us.  If you want to do something stupid, we're not going to stop you anymore than your shell would\".  In other words, if the user is specifying the whole command, there can be no shell injection.  Am I missing something?\n\n[0]: https://docs.python.org/2/library/subprocess.html#frequently-used-arguments","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1520437924,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3MTE4MzQyNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2275#issuecomment-371183427"},"message":"From my point of view, doing this right ultimately leads to treating the shell like a container aka computational environment, which you then need to reference properly, provide version, etc.\nOnly, to then come up with convenience switches and (changing) default values in order to figure out, how to make that beast useable in 90% of the cases.\n\nI'd honestly just go for standard shell (`/bin/sh` for POSIX). If you need sth special - call it! After all it's about reproducibility, so it's okay to require people to specify things.\n\nRe security:\nWhat @kyleam says.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1520439070,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3MTE5MDEyNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2275#issuecomment-371190125"},"message":"\u003e If you need sth special - call it!\n\nThat is where `--no-shell` or `--shell=` should come in then since otherwise it would be a mess with all the escaping if I need to `run /bin/bash -c \"cmd\"` which in turn is then converted to `/bin/sh -c \"/bin/bash -c \\\"...` internally if we cannot forbid having no shell.\nBut indeed may be then it should be `--runner` or something like that, not `--shell`?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1520440183,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3MTE5NjUyNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2275#issuecomment-371196527"},"message":"Well, the escaping mess is a valid point, although I guess, that in most \"real\" cases you'd rather simply call a script which in return specifies the shell to use than having more or less complex commands within the call to `datalad-run`.\n\nI don't  entirely get the idea of  `--runner`. What exactly would you pass in? And how would you combine it with the needs datalad itself has for the Runner, so we use the correct git/annex for the calls datalad run actually is for? Just asking - not sure, whether that's actually easy.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1520441509,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3MTIwNDE2Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2275#issuecomment-371204167"},"message":"not sure... I know that I am so special but here you go -- a dump of the commands I have (tried or succeded) to `datalad run` recently:\n```shell\ndatalad -l 2 run \"find -iname *..n